WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.557
You already know most of this.

2
00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:14.168
Studio 3 gave you the machinery: error analysis before metrics, test sets with provenance, code graders and calibrated judges, intervals instead of anecdotes, and regression gates in CI.

3
00:00:14.448 --> 00:00:25.883
Lesson 11 of that studio introduced agent-specific ideas: grading the end state rather than the agent’s claims, trajectory checks for policy, and pass^k from repeated trials.

4
00:00:26.163 --> 00:00:28.211
This lesson does not repeat those.

5
00:00:28.491 --> 00:00:39.755
It turns them into an eval you can build for the agent in this studio’s project, and adds three things that matter most once an agent has tools: environments, safety gates, and cost.

6
00:00:40.035 --> 00:00:41.805
Start with shared vocabulary.

7
00:00:42.085 --> 00:01:00.880
Anthropic’s agent-evals guide defines a task as one test with defined inputs and success criteria, a trial as one attempt at it, a grader as logic that scores some aspect of a trial (a task can have several), a transcript as the full record of a trial (for the Claude API,

8
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.067
the complete messages array), and the outcome as the final state of the environment.

9
00:01:06.347 --> 00:01:15.328
Its example makes the distinction stick: a booking agent may say the flight is booked, but the outcome is whether a reservation exists in the database.

10
00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:31.203
The guide also offers reassurance that matters for a PM planning the work: teams often delay agent evals because they think they need hundreds of tasks, and it suggests 20 to 50 simple tasks drawn from real failures is a strong start,

11
00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:35.792
because early changes have large effects that small samples can detect.
